<a id="section-api-access"></a>
#API Access
Authentication to the GoSpotCheck API uses OAuth2 and a token is required for access to the API.
OAuth2 tokens are set in the `Authorization` header for your requests as follows:

        Authorization: Bearer abcdef1234567890

Contact your GoSpotCheck Customer Success Manager or [support@gospotcheck.com](mailto:support@gospotcheck.com) for obtaining an access token to the API.

<a id="section-api-help"></a>
#API Help

Have questions or need support?
Email your GoSpotCheck Customer Success Manager or [support@gospotcheck.com](mailto:support@gospotcheck.com).

<a id="section-api-tech-requirements"></a>
# API Technical Requirements
## Protocol and Communication
- `api.gospotcheck.com` is only accessible via HTTPS, using TLS 1.1 or better.
- As of August 1st, 2018 we deprecated the legacy TLS 1.0 endpoint (api-legacy.gospotcheck.com).
If you have questions or concerns about this deprecation, please contact your GoSpotCheck Customer Success Manager or [support@gospotcheck.com](mailto:support@gospotcheck.com).

## Rate Limits
The API enforces the following rate limits. Exceeding these limits will return the following HTTP error code: `403 Forbidden (Rate Limit Exceeded)`
- **Interval Limit** 10 requests per second (must be in serial, concurrent requests cannot be accepted)
- **Daily Limit** 100,000 requests per day
If you need higher limits to support your specific use case, please contact your GoSpotCheck Customer Success Manager or [support@gospotcheck.com](mailto:support@gospotcheck.com)

<a id="section-api-endpoints"></a>
#API Endpoints
The following resources are available for index and CRUD operations:

- `/external/v1/places` - [Places](#group-places-index)
- `/external/v1/place_groups` - [PlaceGroups](#group-placegroups-index)
- `/external/v1/users` - [Users](#group-users-index)
- `/external/v1/teams` - [Teams](#group-teams-index)
- `/external/v1/missions` - [Missions](#group-missions-index)
- `/external/v1/missions/{mission_id}/tasks` - [Tasks](#group-mission-tasks-index) - For the creation of Sections and Tasks
- `/external/v1/mission_responses` - [MissionResponses](#group-missionresponses-index) (read-only)
- `/external/v1/task_responses` - [TaskResponses](#group-taskresponses-index) (read-only)
- `/external/v1/user_place_assignments` - [UserPlaceAssignments](#group-userplaceassignments-index)
- `/external/v1/custom_views` - [CustomViews](#group-customviews-index)
- `/external/v1/catalogs` - [Catalogs](#group-catalogs-index)
- `/external/v1/catalogs/{catalog_id}/items` - [CatalogItems](#group-catalog-items-index) - For the creation, update, deletion of Catalog Items
- `/external/v1/async_jobs/csv_exports/{job_guid}/status` - [AsyncJobs](#group-async-jobs-status) - For querying the status of requested asynchronous jobs

All parameters for a specific endpoint will be described in the appropriate endpoint sections. All 'string' field types have a 255 character limit unless otherwise noted.

<a id="section-v2-endpoints"></a>
#APIv2 Endpoints
The following resources are available for index and CRUD operations:

- `/external/v2/companies/:id/places` - [Places](#v2-places)
- `/external/v2/companies/:id/place_groups` - [Place Groups](#v2-place-groups)
- `/external/v2/companies/:id/image_rec/scene_types/:id/tags` - [Tag Edits](#v2-tags)
- `/external/v2/companies/:id/mission_jobs` - [Mission Jobs](#v2-mission-jobs)

<a id="section-api-requests"></a>
#API Requests

Requests for the GoSpotCheck API are all RESTful, with parameters following the endpoint path.
Each request in the documentation is displayed with the appropriate HTTP method and sample parameters.
The following request using HTTP GET requests all places for your company created after August 7th, 2014
and belong to place group 7:

`http://api.gospotcheck.com/external/v1/places?created_at.gt=1407391200&place_group_id.eq=7`

Data may be sent in parameters or in the request body as JSON.

***Note:*** Requests will time out after 30 seconds.

<a id="section-api-requests-index-requests"></a>
## Index Requests

The index requests on the GoSpotCheck API allow for sorting, paging, and numerous types of matchers depending on the field types for a particular resource. For examples of how to use the different matchers, please see the [Places documentation](#group-places-index).

<a id="section-api-requests-index-sorting"></a>
## Index Sorting

Sorting is done by two parameters in any index request. If `sort_by` appears as
a parameter, `sort_direction` is also required. Currently requests may be sorted by only one field.

| Parameter            | Type                   | Example                |
|----------------------|------------------------|------------------------|
| `sort_by`            | string                 | `sort_by=created_at`   |
| `sort_direction`     | string `asc` or `desc` | `sort_direction=desc`  |

<a id="section-api-requests-index-paging"></a>
## Index Paging

Paging automatically occurs for all index requests. The following parameters allow you to control how many responses are returned.

| Parameter            | Type                               | Example                |
|----------------------|------------------------------------|------------------------|
| `page`               | integer, default: `1`              | `page=31`              |
| `per_page`           | integer, default: `25`, max: `200` | `per_page=100`         |

<a id="section-api-requests-index-filtering"></a>
## Index Filtering

Filtering your GET index results is done by using different operator matchers on different
fields for the current endpoint resource.

NOTE:
- Only AND filtering is supported. There is no support for OR filtering at this time.
- There are six operators available for various types of fields in the system.
- Strings, integers and floats generally may be used for each matcher, depending on your needs.
- Values must be URI encoded by your request client before issuing the request.
- Large requests will time out after 30 seconds.
- When using `.lt`, `.lte`, `.gt`, or `.gte`, be sure to set upper and lower limits. Otherwise, you risk hitting the 30 second time out.

| Matcher   | Matches                         | Example                    | URI Encoded                  |With Range Limits             |
|-----------|---------------------------------|----------------------------|------------------------------|------------------------------|
| `.eq`     | Exact match                     | `name.eq=GSC Headquarters` | `name.eq=GSC%20Headquarters` |                              |
| `.gt`     | Greater than value              | `id.gt=5`                  | `id.gt=5`                    | `id.gt=5&id.lt=10`           |
| `.gte`    | Greather than or equal to value | `id.gte=10`                | `id.gte=10`                  | `id.gte=10&id.lte=100`       |
| `.lt`     | Less than value                 | `id.lt=200`                | `id.lt=200`                  | `id.lt=200&id.gt=100`        |
| `.lte`    | Less than or equal to value     | `id.lte=150`               | `id.lte=150`                 | `id.lte=150&id.gte=50`       |
| `.in`     | Value in comma-separated list   | `id.in=3,14,52,68`         | `id.in=3,14,52,68`           |                              |
 
<a id="section-includes-methods"></a>
## Includes & Methods

For some of the available resources, you are able to request additional data for a given request. A common use case would be to include teams and placegroups when requesting Missions.

- `include` requests bring in data from an associated model
- `methods` requests bring in derived or additional data points from a different model

NOTE:
- The `include` and `methods` parameters are not supported (and are ignored) on an ASYNC request.
- Associated data is not included by default and requires the `include` or `methods` parameter be set.
- Available includes and methods are listed with each endpoint
- Multiple includes may be separated by commas
- Multiple methods may be separated by commas
- Includes and methods can be used in the same request


| Parameter            | Type                               | Example                        |
|----------------------|------------------------------------|--------------------------------|
| `include`            | String, default: ''                | `include=teams,place_groups`   |
| `methods`            | String, default: ''                | `methods=company_id`           |


<a id="section-api-requests-crud-requests"></a>
## CRUD Requests

Create, update and delete requests follow standard RESTful practices. While some fields
are available on a resource, you may not be able to set values such as `created_at` during
a CREATE, UPDATE or DELETE action. The allowed parameters are listed at the beginning of each CRUD
section for a particular resource. For an example, please see the [Places documentation](#group-places-index).

<a id="section-api-requests-creating-multiple"></a>
## Creating Multiple Records in a Single Request

The API provides additional support for creating multiple of each resource in a single request for
the CREATE action as an enhancement. To create multiple records, you must
specify the name of the pluralized resource with underscores (which you can pull from the URL).
For example, to create multiple `PlaceGroups` at the same time, the request parameters must appear as
the following:

            {
                "place_groups": [
                    {
                        "name": "West Coast"
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Rocky Mountains"
                    }
                ]
            }

If you do not include the pluralized, underscorized name in the parameters, the API will assume you
are creating a single record as in a standard RESTful request.

***Note:*** The [Tasks](#group-mission-tasks-index) endpoint does not follow this multiple create pattern.
Please refer to the documentation of the Tasks endpoint to understand how sections and tasks are created.

<a id="section-api-responses"></a>
#API Responses
All API response are formatted in a consistent format, regardless of HTTP status code.

        {
            "request": {
                "status_code": 200,
                "status_message": "OK",
                "path": "/external/v1/places",
                "method": "GET",
                "params": {}
            },
            "paging": {
                "next_page": null,
                "current_page": 1,
                "previous_page": null,
                "per_page": 25,
                "total_records": 18,
                "first_timestamp": 1403156620.0609322,
                "last_timestamp": 1400008286.827591
            },
            "data": {},
            "errors": {}
        }
        
***Note:*** Requests will time out after 30 seconds.

## Response "request" hash
The request hash in a response contains information about the given request that
resulted in this response.

- `status_code` - The HTTP status code for this response
- `status_message` - A human-formatted status message for the response
- `path` - The endpoint path for the given request
- `method` - The HTTP method for the given request
- `params` - A hash of parsed parameter keys and values for the given request

## Response "paging" hash
The paging hash in a response only exists if the data result is an array which appears
during index actions and other specific API endpoint actions.

- `next_page` - Contains the value for the next page. Will be null if no further pages exist.
- `current_page` - The currently requested page. Defaults to `1` if no page is given in the request.
- `previous_page` - The page prior to the current page. Will be null if `current_page` is `1`.
- `per_page` - The maximum amount of records that may be returned in this response. If not given in parameters, defaults to `25`. Max is `200`.
- `total_records` - The total records that match this request, regardless of current paging results.
- `first_timestamp` - The `created_at` timestamp of the first record in the results `data` array.
- `last_timestamp` - The `created_at` timestamp of the last record in the results `data` array.

## Response "data" object
The `data` object in the results hash may be either a hash or an array, depending on the endpoint action.

## Response "errors" hash
The `errors` hash only appears in results when the request `status_code` is in the 400 or 500 range
to inform the client of errors such as incorrect field formats, model validation errors or server errors.

<a id="section-api-metadata"></a>
## Custom Properties (Metadata)

The API supports custom properties for **users**, **places**, **catalog items**, **tasks**, and **missions**. NOT supported for **catalogs**. Custom property **keys** are created
by GoSpotCheck for your company, and once available, they appear as attributes for each *instance* of the resource returned from a request.

Learn more about [GoSpotCheck](https://support.gospotcheck.com/hc/en-us/articles/217899388-Properties-101) Properties.

For example, a place without any custom properties would return as:

        {
            "address":"201 Ashton Way",
            "city":"Camden Town",
            "country":"US",
            "created_at":"2014-08-07T18:26:30-04:00",
            "custom_place_id":null,
            "id":44,
            "name":"A Cool New Place",
            "postal_code":"24315",
            "state":"KY",
            "updated_at":"2014-08-07T18:26:30-04:00"
        }

When the custom properties keys **Region** and **Management District** are created for places for your company, the above place would be returned as:

        {
            "address":"201 Ashton Way",
            "city":"Camden Town",
            "country":"US",
            "created_at":"2014-08-07T18:26:30-04:00",
            "custom_place_id":null,
            "id":44,
            "name":"A Cool New Place",
            "postal_code":"24315",
            "state":"KY",
            "updated_at":"2014-08-07T18:26:30-04:00",
            "region":null,
            "management district":null,
        }

Now each place can save/update its own value of **region** and **management district**. When creating or updating resources that support custom properties, you may simply set the custom properties as attributes to each call
and they will be created or updated appropriately and returned as populated attributes.

**Note 1:** Custom property keys can be created with varying case and spaces as seen in this example, but are expected to be lower-case when used in the API or they will be ignored (spaces are ok).

**Note 2:** Currently the API does not support index filtering on custom properties attributes, and may be added to the API in the future.


<a id="section-async-jobs"></a>
#Asynchronous CSV Jobs

**Note 1:** This feature is currently available on the following endpoints: `places`, `users`, `user_place_assignments`, `mission_responses`, `task_responses`, `missions`, `tasks`, `custom_views`,`catalogs`, and `catalog_items` endpoints.

**Note 2:** This feature is planned for the following endpoints soon! `place_groups`, `teams`

**Note 3:** This feature is currently only available on the **index** action for each enabled endpoint.

By default, all requests to the External API are synchronous and all data requested are returned within the same request/response cycle.
One limitation of this approach is that all INDEX responses are limited to a maximum number of records as defined in the [Index Paging section](#section-api-requests-index-paging).
To enable returning an unlimited number of records, you are able to create a job to be executed asynchronously to return all records that match your current query.

## Async Parameters

Any existing INDEX request on enabled endpoints may be converted into an asynchronous job by simply adding the following parameters:

- `_async=true` - Available values are `true`, and `false`. Defaults to `false` on all enabled endpoints.
- `_delimiter=,` - Available values are `,`, `;`, and `|`. Defaults to `,`.
- `_headers=true` - Available values are `true` and `false`. Defaults to `true`.

### Converting a Synchronous Request into an Asynchronous Job

Given the following request to get places for my company:

```https://api.gospotcheck.com/external/v1/places```

It can be converted into an asynchronous job by adding `_async=true` as a parameter:

```https://api.gospotcheck.com/external/v1/places?_async=true```

NOTE: Includes and methods will be ignored in any async request.


## Response Structure and Return Codes

When an asynchronous job is successfully created, a code of `200` will be returned. Any errors will result in a return code of `422` and error messages will be returned in the standard format.

The response structure of a requested asynchronous job includes a `job_guid` that you use on the [Async Jobs endpoint](#endpoint-async-jobs)
to query for the current status of the endpoint. This structure is almost identical to what is returned from the [Async Jobs endpoint](#endpoint-async-jobs).
Please refer to that endpoint for more details about obtaining the status of your job.

        {
            "request": {
                "status_code": 201,
                "status_message": "AsyncJobsCsvExport created successfully",
                "path": "/external/v1/places",
                "method": "GET",
                "format": "json",
                "params": {
                    "_async": "true"
                }
            },
            "data": {
                "id": 166,
                "job_guid": "3418c079-53dd-49da-a292-e8b6b1c3e443",
                "started_at": null,
                "completed_at": null,
                "failed_at": null,
                "error_type": null,
                "error_message": null,
                "download_url": null,
                "created_at": "2016-10-12T14:15:18.606-04:00",
                "updated_at": "2016-10-12T14:15:18.606-04:00",
                "file_size": null,
                "row_count": null,
                "status_url": "http://api.gospotcheck.com/external/v1/async_jobs/csv_exports/3418c079-53dd-49da-a292-e8b6b1c3e443/status"
            }
        }

## Export File Format

Currently the only file format supported for exporting all records via an asynchronous job is CSV. Once a job is completed, the response
body of the [Async Jobs endpoint](#endpoint-async-jobs) `status` request will return the `download_url` key that is the URL of the CSV.
For example, the above request will result in a file containing example data delimited by the default delimiter, with headers as follows:

`"download_url": "https://gospotcheck-csv-exports.s3.amazonaws.com/3418c079-53dd-49da-a292-e8b6b1c3e443_company_database_view_120161012-3-185q0i2.csv"`

Resulting CSV example:

    id,campaign_id,place_id,user_id,place_state,created_at
    23037,2848,1387270,8440,CO,2016-08-17 17:35:34.914505
    23038,2848,1387270,8440,CO,2016-08-17 17:49:11.850136
    23393,2848,1426954,8440,CO,2016-09-26 16:45:32.190473

## Export File Expiration

Currently all exports are set to expire **one week** after they are created at which point they will be inaccessible.

<a id="section-endpoint-documentation"></a>
#Endpoint Documentation

Documentation specific for each endpoint follows below. You may also use the links on the table on the right sidebar to navigate to any endpoint.

<a id="group-places-index"></a>
# Group Places
The Places endpoint allows you to add, delete, list and update places
for your company.

Remember:
- Places are your accounts or locations where mission responses are completed
- Places can be assigned to a Placegroup after they are created

## Places - Base Attributes
| Field                    | Type | Notes   |
|-----------------------------|-----|-----------|
|`id`                     |  int  | Unique GSC id  |
|`name`                     |  string  | Name of place, not unique |
|`address`                     |  string  | Street number and name - e.g. 123 Main St |
|`city`                     |  string  | City in which the place exists - e.g. Denver |
|`state`                     |  string  | State in which the place exists - e.g. CO or Colorado |
|`postal_code`                     |  string  | Zip or postal code in which the place exists - e.g. 80202 |
|`country`                     |  string  | Country in which the place exists, formatted according to the [ISO3166-1 alpha-2](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ISO_3166-1_alpha-2) standard - e.g. US |
|`created_at`                     |  datetime  | When place was created in GSC   |
|`updated_at`                     |  datetime  | When place was last updated in GSC |
|`custom_place_id`                     |  string  | Your unique place identifier - e.g. TDLinx or Salesforce Account ID |
|`status`                     |  string  | Enabled places will appear in the mobile app, disabled places will not. Enabled places have a null status; disabled places have a status of 'disabled'. |
|`disabled_at`                     |  datetime  | If place is disabled (or has ever been disabled), field will display when it was last disabled  |
|`geocoding_disabled`                     |  boolean  | Indicates whether the place will be re-geocoded the next time it is updated - defaults to false |
|`metadata/properties`                     |  string  | Custom key/value pairs, key must be created by GSC but values can be pulled from the API |

## Available Includes for Places
For more info, see: [Includes & Methods](#section-includes-methods)

- `place_groups`
- `user_place_assignments`
- `assigned_users`

## Available Methods for Places
For more info, see: [Includes & Methods](#section-includes-methods)

- `place_group_ids`
- `company_id`

## Places [/external/v1/places]

### List all Places for company [GET]

+ Request (application/json)

    + Headers

            Authorization: Bearer 1234567890abcdef

+ Response 200 (application/json)

        {
            "request": {
                "status_code":200,
                "status_message":"OK",
                "path":"/external/v1/places",
                "method":"GET",
                "params":{}
            },
            "paging": {
                "next_page":null,
                "current_page":1,
                "previous_page":null,
                "per_page":25,
                "total_records":204,
                "first_timestamp":1403156620.0609322,
                "last_timestamp":1400008286.827591
            },
            "data":[
                {
                    "id": 1427912,
                    "name": "GoSpotCheck",
                    "address": "1520 Market St",
                    "city": "Denver",
                    "state": "CO",
                    "postal_code": "80202",
                    "country": "US",
                    "created_at": "2017-07-17T16:36:34.464-04:00",
                    "updated_at": "2017-07-17T16:36:34.464-04:00",
                    "custom_place_id": "example123",
                    "status": null,
                    "disabled_at": null,
                    "geocoding_disabled": false,
                    "place_property": "Place Property Value XYZ"
                },
                ...
            ]
        }


### Create a new Place or Multiple Places [POST]

The following attributes are permitted when creating a Place. All other attributes will be ignored.

| Field             | Type           | Required    | Notes  |
|-------------------|----------------|-------------|--------|
|`name`             | string         | Yes         | |
|`address`          | string         | Yes         | |
|`city`             | string         | Yes         | |
|`state`            | string         | Yes         | |
|`postal_code`      | string         | Yes          | |
|`country`          | string         | Yes          | |
|`coordinates`      | string         | Maybe       | Required if longitude/latitude are blank|
|`latitude`         | float          | Maybe       | Required if coordinates is blank|
|`longitude`        | float          | Maybe       | Required if coordinates is blank|
|`country`          | string         | Yes         | |
|`custom_place_id`  | int            | No          | |
|`status`           | string         | No          | Leave blank for active/enabled. Include 'disabled' to disable place.|

+ Request - Create One New Place (application/json)

    + Headers

            Authorization: Bearer 1234567890abcdef

    + Body

            {
                "name": "A Cool New Place",
                "address": "201 Ashton Way",
                "city": "Camden Town",
                "state": "KY",
                "postal_code": "24315",
                "country": "US"
            }

+ Response 201 - Create One New Place (application/json)

        {
            "request": {
                "status_code": 201,
                "status_message": "Place created successfully",
                "path": "/external/v1/places",
                "method": "POST",
                "format": "json",
